# Video Motion Rules

Use this reference for camera movement, pacing, transitions, sound, Seedance prompts, continuity, and final video planning.

## Aspect Ratio Rule

Only use these aspect ratios unless the user explicitly overrides:

- `9:16` vertical for Reels, TikTok, Stories, Shorts, and mobile-first ads. This is the user's primary format.
- `16:9` horizontal for sales decks, websites, YouTube, client presentations, and cinematic horizontal films.

At the beginning of a new project, ask which aspect ratio to use: `9:16` or `16:9`. If the user says Reels, Instagram, Shorts, TikTok, Stories, social, or mobile, default to `9:16` and state it. If the user says presentation, website, YouTube, sales deck, or horizontal film, default to `16:9` and state it.

The selected aspect ratio controls every generated image, storyboard frame, clean keyframe, and video prompt.

## Storytelling And Retention

The complete video must tell a story. Do not create random camera moves over unrelated rooms, facades, or amenities.

Every video should have:

- a visual hook in the first 3 seconds;
- a clear promise, reveal, transformation, question, or emotional reason to keep watching;
- a progression that helps the viewer understand, desire, or imagine using the project;
- continuity anchors such as material, light, line, water, facade rhythm, plan logic, character action, or site movement;
- a closing beat that reinforces sales value.

For social videos, make the first 3 seconds more visually striking than the rest, but keep architecture readable.

## Pacing

For Reels, TikTok, Stories, and high-retention sales videos, do not default to 5 seconds per scene.

- Normal architecture/detail shots: 1.5-3 seconds.
- Quick hooks, match cuts, feature flashes, and material inserts: 1-2 seconds.
- Complex transformations, construction progressions, room-to-room reveals, or character actions: 4-5 seconds.
- Closing hero shot: 2-4 seconds unless the user asks for a calm premium ending.

For a 30-second social video, prefer 8-14 shots. Use 5-second clips only when the shot truly needs time to read or quality would suffer.

## Movement Selection

Movement variety must serve the story, architecture, sales message, or retention hook.

- Choose movement according to the beat: curiosity, arrival, material value, comfort, indoor-outdoor connection, lifestyle, or final hero reveal.
- Keep movement coherent across the full film.
- Do not stack many movement styles in the same short shot unless it is intentionally a hook or transition.
- Preserve architectural readability: the viewer must understand facade, layout, material, garden, furniture, water, or room scale.
- If fast motion is used, include a brief stable settle on the hero composition before cutting.

Use `references/camera-movement-library.md` for movement vocabulary and safety tiers.

## Safe Camera And Transition Language

Prefer:

- slow push-in;
- pull-back reveal;
- lateral dolly;
- crane up/down;
- tilt from material detail to volume;
- pan across facade rhythm;
- drone approach from the same visible angle;
- parallax through foreground landscaping;
- match cut by light, line, material, water, glass, or horizon;
- hard cut for amenity montage;
- slow dissolve only for calm luxury mood.

Avoid:

- flying through walls unless the reference supports a planned transition;
- orbiting around unseen sides;
- entering rooms not shown;
- changing day to night unless conceptual risk is accepted;
- using reflections/mirrors/water as the main continuity device unless essential.

## Continuous Start/End Frame Mode

Use this mode only when requested or when it clearly improves continuity.

Valid patterns:

- Single continuous clip: one video covers a continuous movement over 4-10 seconds with one clear camera path and environment.
- Chained clips: each clip has a start keyframe and end keyframe. End frame N becomes start frame N+1.

Rules:

- Preserve architecture, lighting, materials, furniture, people, and camera direction across handoffs.
- Use the same exact image for `end frame N` and `start frame N+1`.
- Label repeated handoff frames as technical continuity frames, not repeated storyboard shots.
- Avoid jumps in lens, height, angle, time of day, or room position unless intentionally a match cut.

## Sound Design

Do not ask generated video tools to add music unless the user explicitly requests music.

By default, request only action-related sound effects and natural ambient sound: water/pool/cascade, birds, coastal wind, leaves, stone/wood/door foley, quiet premium interior room tone, and subtle whooshes tied to camera movement.

Add negative audio when relevant: `no music, no soundtrack, no song, no beat, no melody, no vocals, no different background music per clip`.

## Seedance Prompt Rules

Generate the final Seedance prompt only after the user shares the generated storyboard board, clean keyframes, or explicitly asks for a provisional prompt.

Begin with references:

```text
REFERENCES:
- storyboard-board.png: approved storyboard board and timeline reference.
- exact-file-name.png: role/name.
```

Then instruct Seedance to follow the storyboard sequence while animating the real architectural scene. The video should not look like a camera filming a storyboard page.

Keep the prompt compact:

- duration and aspect ratio;
- strict storyboard-following instruction;
- architectural continuity anchors;
- photoreal real-camera target;
- camera and edit rhythm;
- clean keyframe or original render reference for each important clip;
- close-up/crop target when using a detail from a larger reference;
- start/end frame references when using continuous mode;
- critical negative constraints.

## Manual-Derived Start/End Keyframe Planning

When a requested video depends on creative transitions or process storytelling, plan still images before final Seedance prompts.

Valid patterns:

- `single-reference derivative`: one approved image generates a safe close-up, technical overlay, mood overlay, or nearby supported keyframe.
- `multi-reference composite`: approved image plus plan, site map, moodboard, material board, or construction reference.
- `transformation pair`: start frame and end frame designed for one Seedance clip.
- `chained progression`: end frame N becomes start frame N+1 for timelapse, loteamento, location arrival, or room-to-room movement.

Use start/end mode for:

- raw site to highlighted lot;
- aerial to masterplan;
- facade to technical drawing;
- facade to exploded massing;
- wide interior to detail insert;
- render to material mood overlay;
- construction stage to construction stage;
- gate/daytime to night hero, only when requested.

Before writing Seedance prompts, verify:

- all frames share the selected aspect ratio;
- source references and generated keyframes are approved or clearly provisional;
- architecture, site boundaries, materials, furniture, landscape, and lighting are consistent;
- repeated frames are labeled as technical handoff frames;
- the clip can be understood as a realistic camera/edit transition, not a random morph.

